    "innovation": "This paper demonstrates that multidecadal sea surface temperature variability in the western tropical Pacific is remotely forced by the Atlantic multidecadal oscillation through a teleconnection mechanism involving a subtropical North Pacific bridge and local cloud-radiation positive feedback.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic multidecadal oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "western tropical Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "Observational analysis suggests that the western tropical Pacific (WTP) sea surface temperature (SST) shows predominant variability over multidecadal time scales, which is unlikely to be explained by the Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation. Here we show that this variability is largely explained by the remote Atlantic multidecadal oscillation (AMO).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic multidecadal oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The AMO warm SST anomaly generates an atmospheric teleconnection to the North Pacific, which weakens the Aleutian low and subtropical North Pacific westerlies.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "El Ni\u00f1o-Southern Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "East Asia",
            "target_entity_type": "Continent",
            "paragraph": "At interannual time scales, there is a strong simultaneous relationship between the WTP SST and El Ni\u00f1o-Southern Oscillation (ENSO) in winter, and the WTP SST anomalies play an important role in conveying the delayed impact of ENSO on summer climate over East Asia.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Atlantic multidecadal oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "North Atlantic",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The persistent basin-scale SST anomalies associated with the AMO have significant impacts on the climate not only in and around the North Atlantic, but also in remote regions.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "Interdecadal Pacific Oscillation",
            "source_entity_type": "Teleconnection",
            "relation": "INFLUENCES_REGION",
            "target_entity_instance": "Pacific",
            "target_entity_type": "No_Country_Region",
            "paragraph": "The IPO shows fluctuations over decadal to multidecadal timescales and is significant correlated with a number of climate indices around the Pacific.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
